      "type": "done",
      "answer": "Based on the current lecture, the two forms of locality are defined and illustrated as follows:\n\n**Temporal Locality**\n- Definition: \"A piece of data is quickly reused in the future.\" [S1]\n- Example given: **Loops** — the same data item is accessed repeatedly over time. [S1]\n- Concrete illustration: In the code `for (i = 0; i < 100; i++) { C[i] = A[i] * B[i] + D; }`, the variable `D` is \"reused every iteration,\" which is labeled as temporal locality. [S3]\n\n**Spatial Locality**\n- Definition: \"The neighbors of the current data access tend to be accessed in the future.\" [S1]\n- Example given: **Arrays** — nearby memory locations are accessed close together in time. [S1]\n- Concrete illustration: In the same loop, `A`, `B`, and `C` are \"used in sequence\" (e.g., C[0], C[1], C[2], …), which is labeled as spatial locality. The lecture notes that a cache line is \"fetch[ed] once, cache[d]\" to exploit this. [S3]\n\n**Key distinction (reasoning):**\n- Temporal locality is about **reusing the same data** at a later time (time dimension) — e.g., a loop-carried variable like `D`. [S3]\n- Spatial locality is about **accessing neighboring data** after the current access (space/adjacency dimension) — e.g., sequential array elements. [S3]\n\nBoth are described as the \"hot feature\" that makes caches useful, and the lecture states that high-speed caches are used to \"capture localities … to address memory wall.\" [S2]\n\nSources: page 22 [S1], page 23 [S2], page 24 [S3].",
